#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179531 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3be2c44e-775f-53fa-a078-cff25eaea903" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/id #uuid "7cec344e-596a-51ce-87cc-d0e44dc48df4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7118" :question/problem "Construct a definite integral that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C (x^2+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x + x\\,\\mathrm{d}y,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t) = t\\,\\mathbf{i} + t^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in\\left[0,1\\right].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad\ny(t) = t^2.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(({x}^{2}+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\cdot 1+t\\cdot 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179591 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5ecc45-95ec-55c0-86bf-bbaeffef4049" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X, Y, and Z"}] :question/id #uuid "2b0b2d1f-df55-56cf-9ae9-ccef499e2352" :question/math-academy-id "e-9517" :question/problem "Construct a definite integral that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C x^3\\,\\mathrm{d}x + z\\,\\mathrm{d}y + y\\,\\mathrm{d}z,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix $\\mathbf{r}(t)=2t\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos t\\,\\mathbf{j} + \\sin t\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in[0,\\pi]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) =2t, \\qquad \ny(t) = \\cos t , \\qquad \nz(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{3}\\,dx+z\\,dy+y\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+y\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2t)^{3}\\cdot (2)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }16{t}^{3}-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }16{t}^{3}+({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }16{t}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179596 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2daf57e1-a02d-5e5f-b4a4-bb8ae9638a75" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "c18fbc4a-24d5-517d-9a3c-949a11787076" :question/math-academy-id "e-9528" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C y\\,\\mathrm{d}x -x\\,\\mathrm{d}y ,$ where $C$ is a portion of the ellipse parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 3\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in[0,\\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny(t) = 3\\sin t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-3{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,dt \\\\  & =-3{t|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-3\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3be2c44e-775f-53fa-a078-cff25eaea903" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f99d644-a1ad-5b43-b8be-36588f2a8d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{2}2t-1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413006} {:answer/id #uuid "5adc34ca-ff1e-5a7e-a58d-e5f402ec7226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{2}1+t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413013} {:answer/id #uuid "82a266c0-edf7-5c04-bf6f-54846fdc04bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32{\\int }_{0}^{1}1+t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413019} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d23f52-8e6e-5e44-9af4-4b175eff035c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{1}1-t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413033} {:answer/id #uuid "ee463fff-4400-5fdd-a30d-2b00697809d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32{\\int }_{0}^{1}1-t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee463fff-4400-5fdd-a30d-2b00697809d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32{\\int }_{0}^{1}1-t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413034} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9bb65a0-9776-5bce-a475-1e502f4ca7a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb63c8a6-afef-5638-9fea-8aaeb2054ae5" :question/math-academy-id "q-124594" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(x-y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=4t\\,i+(4-4t)\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=4t,\\,y(t)=4-4t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-4t)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i+(-4)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(x-y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+(x-y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x-y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4t+(4-4t))\\cdot 4+(4t-(4-4t))\\cdot (-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16t+16-16t-16t+16-16t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}32-32t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{0}^{1}1-t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5ecc45-95ec-55c0-86bf-bbaeffef4049" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X, Y, and Z"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "207e611c-1715-5848-8636-a148354824ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}8{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}+3t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413007} {:answer/id #uuid "5653a3c0-a432-54b1-bd34-3f7fd032cf28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}4{t}^{4}-6{t}^{2}+2\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413012} {:answer/id #uuid "7491be96-5601-5301-a5ca-7f7629fc7e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413017} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d59a8c-93ee-5534-b2f2-ae231427b12e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}16{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413029} {:answer/id #uuid "ccbb774b-4bad-5678-9592-475093dad980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}4{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2d59a8c-93ee-5534-b2f2-ae231427b12e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}16{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413029} :answer-field/id #uuid "f678fd71-00e1-594a-91b3-8c9260903977" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c8aeb24-90cc-5de9-a270-6cd27812a200" :question/math-academy-id "q-124867" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+{z}^{3}\\,dy-{y}^{2}\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the line $r(t)=\\sqrt{2}t\\,i+2t\\,j+2t\\,k$ for $t\\in [1,4]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\sqrt{2}t,\\,y(t)=2t,\\,z(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+{z}^{3}\\,dy-{y}^{2}\\,dz & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+{z}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt-{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+{z}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{2}t\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})+8{t}^{3}\\cdot (2)-4{t}^{2}\\cdot (2)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}16{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3be2c44e-775f-53fa-a078-cff25eaea903" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355201fd-a87c-5acd-a773-040c3348da1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)+t+1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413008} {:answer/id #uuid "707c6361-9302-5d0e-9948-6d00df2a56db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413016} {:answer/id #uuid "965cf5f1-7747-53da-b55f-f89432ec4b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}1+t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413024} {:answer/id #uuid "a41bf204-b1a4-53a0-b0d0-dbf22f8c37f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413026} {:answer/id #uuid "c32393e5-5f10-5c78-bd47-75d6d938a51b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}t+{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "965cf5f1-7747-53da-b55f-f89432ec4b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}1+t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413024} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f274490-0e74-56cf-8700-86afd0ca19e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a86d0f21-fc9f-5f42-9338-116e42667bb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-124868" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1,\\,y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+(x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)\\cdot 0+(1+t)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}1+t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5ecc45-95ec-55c0-86bf-bbaeffef4049" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X, Y, and Z"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "407b1421-300f-5650-8cf7-dd126bc29f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413010} {:answer/id #uuid "8c8a1d3b-7cc7-56d2-8a0b-b470fcd35f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1+{t}^{2}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413021} {:answer/id #uuid "901a2e65-5fa9-5165-ba1b-6642336cd921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413022} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d6414c-0388-584f-92ec-491360dc6f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{t}^{2}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413028} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1d323b-e5df-5e29-88d7-6527a8a633a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{t}^{2}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd1d323b-e5df-5e29-88d7-6527a8a633a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{t}^{2}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413035} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e33dea3-f3c2-5a02-9f0a-3fc23f07af84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74ba859e-96c2-518a-a67a-a55e65c7d804" :question/math-academy-id "q-39681" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+{z}^{2}\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)=t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+{z}^{2}\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+{z}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+{z}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+{t}^{2}\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{t}^{2}\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2daf57e1-a02d-5e5f-b4a4-bb8ae9638a75" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ea3a4f2-a959-540f-86d2-a526fb567a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413009} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf6010e-a80e-5fc9-aa97-fc8be288477c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413015} {:answer/id #uuid "7acff49a-3d77-5139-87b8-23f8a83a61d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413018} {:answer/id #uuid "874aecd7-c4bb-5970-a264-ff44eec6444f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413020} {:answer/id #uuid "d33fa25d-bc45-54e8-8056-40b5e874ab27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "874aecd7-c4bb-5970-a264-ff44eec6444f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413020} :answer-field/id #uuid "516d65af-e99b-58a7-afb3-db833a18796f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e1c746b-98be-5a3b-93f3-3c945fad75be" :question/math-academy-id "q-90723" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}(3x+y)\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is a portion of the parabola parametrized by $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+(4t-1)\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2},\\,y(t)=4t-1.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+4\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(3x+y)\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3x+y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}((3x+y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3{t}^{2}+4t-1)\\cdot 2t+{t}^{2}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}6{t}^{3}+8{t}^{2}-2t+4{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}6{t}^{3}+12{t}^{2}-2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{4}+4{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{4}+4(2)^{3}-{2}^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =[24+32-4] \\\\  & =52\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2daf57e1-a02d-5e5f-b4a4-bb8ae9638a75" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41ad462e-980a-55e7-8603-ff255c0b0f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (1-\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413011} {:answer/id #uuid "5b212ba5-4523-505f-abd1-6ed94aa4f312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413014} {:answer/id #uuid "95804867-920f-57f6-9af2-84579578a567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (2+\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413023} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee67649-cfe6-55fe-8d15-2b91a6e5a085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (1+\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413025} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b321de-ff7e-5fef-afd8-96961b2c148e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ee67649-cfe6-55fe-8d15-2b91a6e5a085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (1+\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413025} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a11798a-13e6-56d1-93c3-1b2f2fdacf74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "428d05f1-93a8-5aa4-b7d0-699c4ffafa60" :question/math-academy-id "q-90724" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)=t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1+t\\,dt \\\\  & =(t+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi +\\frac{(2\\pi )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi (1+\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
